---
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';

const docs = {
  deployment: withBase('/docs/deployment/'),
  migration: `${site.github}/blob/main/crates/microwave-migrate/README.md`,
  mcp: withBase('/docs/mcp/'),
  readme: `${site.github}#readme`,
};
---
<section id="under-the-hood" class="section under-section reveal">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">For the engineers</p>
      <h2>Under the hood.</h2>
      <p>
        The short version for the person doing due diligence: what runs, what
        stores the data, how authentication works, and where the edges are.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="under-list">
      <details class="under-item">
        <summary>
          <span class="under-index" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
          <span>One binary, two databases.</span>
          <span class="under-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="under-body">
          <p>
            SQLite is the default metadata store and runs in-process. PostgreSQL
            is supported through the same metadata contract when you need a
            server-backed database. Documents are stored transparently
            compressed, and snapshots move between backends.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a class="text-link" href={docs.deployment}>Deployment guide <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="under-item">
        <summary>
          <span class="under-index" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
          <span>Auth that matches.</span>
          <span class="under-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="under-body">
          <p>
            Signed requests use the same canonicalization as Chef Infra Server:
            X-Ops-Sign 1.0, 1.1, and 1.3, with RSA signing and verification.
            Keys are reassembled from the request, legacy password hashes still
            verify, and ACL checks recurse through groups.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a class="text-link" href={docs.readme}>Read the API notes <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="under-item">
        <summary>
          <span class="under-index" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
          <span>Cookbooks and policy.</span>
          <span class="under-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="under-body">
          <p>
            Sandboxes, bookshelf uploads, cookbook resolution, policyfiles, and
            policy groups are implemented. Cookbook blobs can stay on the local
            object store or move to S3-compatible storage such as Garage, with
            presigned URLs for clients.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a class="text-link" href={docs.readme}>Cookbook details <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="under-item">
        <summary>
          <span class="under-index" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
          <span>Search your way.</span>
          <span class="under-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="under-body">
          <p>
            Embedded search is the default and uses a relational scan. Larger
            installations can point Microwave at OpenSearch or Elasticsearch.
            Reindexing rebuilds remote documents from the relational store, and
            result ACL filtering applies before pagination.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a class="text-link" href={docs.readme}>Search notes <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="under-item">
        <summary>
          <span class="under-index" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
          <span>Deploy your way.</span>
          <span class="under-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="under-body">
          <p>
            Run it with Docker, Compose, systemd, or the Debian package. TLS can
            terminate in the server, with SIGHUP certificate reload, or in front
            of it. Object storage and search can be external.
          </p>
          <p>
            Documented topologies are single-node or external-backend;
            multi-replica HA and automated failover are not yet validated.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a class="text-link" href={docs.deployment}>Topologies <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="under-item">
        <summary>
          <span class="under-index" aria-hidden="true">06</span>
          <span>Move in.</span>
          <span class="under-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="under-body">
          <p>
            Import a knife ec backup, crawl a live legacy server through its
            API, or move a Microwave snapshot between SQLite and PostgreSQL.
            There is no automatic <code>chef-server.rb</code> importer;
            configuration is translated with the deployment guide.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a class="text-link" href={docs.migration}>Migration guide <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="under-item">
        <summary>
          <span class="under-index" aria-hidden="true">07</span>
          <span>Agents in the kitchen.</span>
          <span class="under-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="under-body">
          <p>
            MCP is a Streamable HTTP endpoint per organization, authenticated
            with a per-organization bearer token that maps to a Chef principal.
            Read tools cover nodes, environments, cookbooks, runs, and converge
            errors; write tools cover nodes, environments, and cookbook
            versions.
          </p>
          <p>
            Writes are on by default per organization and switch off with{' '}
            <code>writes = false</code>. They reuse the HTTP API's ACL,
            normalization, and validation paths. The default principal is the
            superuser and bypasses ACLs, so a scoped user is the production
            setting.
          </p>
          <p>
            Tool calls emit one structured log line per call, with organization,
            principal, tool, duration, and result count; writes also log the
            target, and server failures add a separate error line. There is no
            durable audit table. The surface is otherwise experimental: no
            token expiry, per-tool scope, or UI.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a class="text-link" href={docs.mcp}>MCP guide <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
